 ## Overview
-NeRF in the Wild (NeRF-W) addresses the failure of standard NeRF optimization on unconstrained photo collections where lighting varies significantly and transient objects (e.g., pedestrians, vehicles) appear inconsistently across images [S1, unofficial][S2]. It differs from its siblings by explicitly decomposing the scene representation into a static radiance field and per-image latent codes for appearance and transient artifacts, rather than relying on adversarial regularization [Aug-NeRF](10294-aug-nerf.md) or model pruning [NeRF Ensemble with Conjoint Pruning](12608-nerf-ensemble-with-conjoint-pruning.md).
+NeRF in the Wild (NeRF-W) extends standard neural radiance fields to handle unconstrained photo collections by decomposing the scene into a static background and transient foreground elements, while accounting for appearance variations [S1, unofficial][S2]. It differs from standard NeRF by introducing per-image latent codes to model lighting changes and transient geometry, allowing it to reconstruct landmarks from internet photos where lighting and content vary significantly [S2][S3, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-NeRF-W extends the canonical NeRF MLP by introducing two additional input embeddings: an appearance embedding to model lighting/viewing variations and a transient embedding to model per-image geometric outliers [S3, unofficial].
+NeRF-W modifies the standard NeRF architecture by adding two per-image latent embeddings to the input of the final MLP layers.
 
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A set of $N$ images $\{I_i\}_{i=1}^N$ with known camera poses $P_i$.
-*   **Output:** A static radiance field $\mathbf{F}_{static}$ and per-image latent codes $\mathbf{a}_i$ (appearance) and $\mathbf{t}_i$ (transient).
